<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">National Liberation Front of Angola</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1954">1954</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as FNLA</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-algeria">National Liberation Front</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> (FNLA) is an Angolan nationalist movement led for decades by Holden Roberto, with its base among the Bakongo of the north. Formed from earlier Bakongo movements and renamed in 1962, it fought Portuguese rule from bases in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaire">Zaire</a> after the rising of 1961. After independence in 1975 it lost the struggle for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpla">MPLA</a> and declined into a minor party.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-of-angola.jpg" alt="National Liberation Front of Angola" width="320" height="325" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dahn · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FNLA_logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The movement began in 1954 as the United People of Northern Angola, a separatist movement of the Bakongo that wished to re-establish their 16th-century feudal kingdom and also protested against forced labour. Holden Roberto was to be its king. It later took the name União das Populações de Angola under Roberto. In March 1961 it began an uprising in the north that killed thousands of white settlers and their servants, as well as assimilados and members of other peoples. The Portuguese sent soldiers, more than 50,000 people died in the violence by the end of 1961, and more than a million refugees were said to have fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaire">Zaire</a>.</p><p>It then merged with the Partido Democratico de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> to form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-front-czechoslovakia">National Front</a> for the Liberation of Angola. By February 1962 it had joined a government in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinshasa">Kinshasa</a>, with Roberto as president and Jonas Savimbi as foreign minister, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organisation-of-african-unity">Organisation of African Unity</a> recognised as Angola's only freedom movement until 1971. Savimbi left in 1964 to form <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unita">UNITA</a>, citing Roberto's dictatorial leadership.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> began aiding the FNLA in 1961, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>, Israel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and others supported it over the years. After the coup in Portugal in April 1974 the FNLA, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpla">MPLA</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unita">UNITA</a> competed for control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>. They signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvor-agreement">Alvor Agreement</a> on 15 January 1975, which set independence for 11 November and a coalition government. Within 24 hours fighting broke out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a>, and the FNLA was expelled from the city.</p><p>From late January 1975 the CIA funded the FNLA, and the aid rose to $32 million for the FNLA and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unita">UNITA</a> by September. South African forces entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> in Operation Savannah. On 10 November 1975 Roberto ordered an assault on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a> through Quifangondo, unaware that the Cubans had reinforced the position, and it failed disastrously. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpla">MPLA</a> kept Luanda, and Agostinho Neto declared the People's Republic of Angola on 11 November. The Clark Amendment ended covert US aid on 19 December 1975, and São Salvador, the FNLA headquarters, fell on 15 February 1976.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After a pact of June 1978 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaire">Zaire</a>, Roberto was exiled to Gabon in November 1979. Remnants fought on as the FNLA-COMIRA until 1983. Before the first multiparty elections of 1992 the FNLA reorganised as a political party, winning 2.4 per cent of the vote and five seats.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “National Liberation Front of Angola” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nationalist revolt began in 1961, with the MPLA, <b>FNLA</b> and UNITA fighting Portugal.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-angola">Portuguese Angola</a></li></ul></section>
